GenDrake runs: a definition crosses a transform domain and comes out as its surface

Three commits ago the generator domains existed as structure with empty member lists, and the
record listed what the forest had shown a transform needs to RUN: types on the target side, a
receiver, an actor. 6f5a8bb gave DrakeTarget its Surface and GenDrake its conversion. This is the
rest, and the gate that fires it.

THE THREE MEMBERS

    gendrake.Emit               rule    var definition TypeDefinition -> fix emission Emission(definition); insert
    generator.SurfaceReceived   rule    var surface draketarget.Surface -> received.add(surface)
    gendrake.Emitter            actor   messageType TypeDefinition; par received; Knowledge from its chain

Emit is the morphism as the engine sees it: a definition in working memory becomes an Emission, which
IS a Surface. It wraps the conversion Emission, which wraps Drake.emit; nothing in the engine changed.

SurfaceReceived is in the SUPER-DOMAIN, by Dev's decision, for the forest's reason: Forest owns
BirchAlarmReceived so that both species' actors see it, and here a Surface arriving is the same event
whichever transform produced it. It is generator.Generator's first member and its reason to exist as
a domain rather than a package. The rule is not decoration — a fact inserted by a rule's action gets
no working-memory node unless something consumes it, and the forest was silent until that rule
existed. Its body is BirchAlarmReceived's, tree-form: `ctx.getRuntime parameters .get parameters par
"received"` then `received.add`.

Emitter is Draco's own actor shape — `factory ActorType` with a construction parameter, start sets it
on the session, message inserts and fires, signal closes — with the DEFAULT activation mode rather
than Draco's CONTINUOUS, because this is a chain: Emit's output is what SurfaceReceived matches.
actorKnowledge walked the chain unasked: its Knowledge accepts Emit and draco.generator.SurfaceReceived,
which is what 87a2bb9 built `super` to do.

THE GATE, AND WHAT ITS ORACLE IS

GenDrakeTest fires the same two rules by hand — no actor system, as the forest's gate does — over
every src/main definition, and asserts that the received surfaces ARE the corpus: the set of
normalized `.drake` files equals the set of normalized Surface values. The oracle is the files, not
Drake.emit. Comparing to Drake.emit would be circular (Emission calls it); the files are what
DrakeGenTest pins Drake.emit TO, so a surface that matches the file is a surface the rule-driven path
produced exactly as the hand-written emitter would have. The two files authored ahead of the emitter
are left out of the insert, as DrakeGenTest leaves them out — so 88 of 90.

Sets rather than a per-definition match, because a Surface has no identity: it is the text and
nothing else. That is honest about what the target type is today, and it is the next thing the
structure lacks — a product that knows which definition it is the surface OF is what a receiver
needs before it can write anything anywhere. The receiver collects; it does not sink.

THE HAZARD FIRED

1cb3578's record named a latent hazard: a file importing both draco._ and draco.generator._ would find
a bare \`Generator\` ambiguous. The first build of this commit showed it is worse than ambiguity.
SurfaceReceived lives IN draco.generator, and its projection said \`Domain[Generator]\` — meaning its
container, the super-domain. But \`import draco._\` is a wildcard import, and in Scala a wildcard import
outranks a member of the file's own package defined in another file. So \`Generator\` silently meant
\`draco.Generator[T]\`, the root transform type, and the compiler said "takes type parameters".
generator/Generator.scala itself compiled only because its object is in the same file.

derivationRef already solves this for a parent on the extends clause — isAmbiguousBare probes every
package in the file's unqualified scope for the simple name and qualifies when another one holds it.
The container reference did not go through it. Now it does: containerName applies the same probe, so
a leaf whose container's name is shadowed says \`Domain[draco.generator.Generator]\`. A corpus scan
found exactly one such leaf today (SurfaceReceived; the self-domain path stays bare, since inside its
own file the name is its own). The .scala is spliced by hand to the predicted line, as the fix cannot
be compiled from the session; DracoGenTest checks it.

WHAT DID NOT CHANGE, AND WHAT IS NEXT

GenDrake.generator, the function, still stands beside Emit, and CLI still calls it. It retires when
the CLI goes through the actor — which needs the sink, which needs identity on the product. Then
GenScala, whose rules are the 43 dispatch sites, on the shape this commit proved on the smaller case.

